{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1046 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d52dc15ef3659a52a6abc0701fa7193960a7f5d7a93e8ceea574f9dbd9499269" :edb/db-before-t 1045 :edb/db-after-t 1046 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534358 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:36:17.579Z" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164949 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164950 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164951 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164952 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420172 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420174 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420179 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164948 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420182 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164948 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420179 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164954 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164955 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164956 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164957 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420175 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420178 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420180 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420181 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186164953 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420180 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186166572 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/76/766b5034dc8f2dd6f3eb8e87f22f5156f58e9ab3aaebd90fa8770d6912d46e67.png)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186166572 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/9d/9d4ae844370b06d82ce892e052ecd1c114519a6bb31e153e3bcb10c2c4806d56.svg)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186166573 1071 "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/4f/4f5284fb6fed9d16cafa85a546e65db1b9ebf5a5211d870b24dfe87bb1ccb49b.svg)" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186166573 1071 "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/ee/ee53aca390d2803fa92f3239dc7548792c6be9e4c05a102fe936aeb9e7ced6d6.png)" 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186166957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420184 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186166992 1050 1020.0 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186167288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186167288 1071 "Let $F(x) = 2x + 3$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.$(4(-1)^{n})/(πn^{2})$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186167288 1071 "Let $F(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186167288 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186168044 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186168044 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186314364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420177 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186314364 1078 #edb/ref 17592186312860 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186314364 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420177 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186372778 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186372778 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186372786 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420176 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186372786 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372787 13194139534358 17592186045417 false] [17592186372786 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420176 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420172 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420172 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420172 1083 #uuid "03ab0d3f-c0ec-5341-aee5-a68517e3ea54" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420173 1080 "9e^{9}" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420173 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420173 1083 #uuid "15bb385e-7f13-50d3-937e-b59ebadbf90b" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420174 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420174 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534358 17592186045417 true] [17592186420174 1083 #uuid "3ef189ae-b8a9-5aad-a7b1-aabed0